<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">ARPANET switches to TCP/IP, the birth of the modern internet</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">On this 'flag day' the US defence research network ARPANET required all hosts to use the TCP/IP protocols. The common standard allowed separate networks to interconnect, forming the internet.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Apple announces the Lisa computer</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Apple unveiled the Lisa, a personal computer with a graphical interface and a mouse. At nearly 10,000 dollars it sold poorly, but its ideas fed directly into the Macintosh a year later.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-26">26 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Stanislav Petrov judges a Soviet missile alert a false alarm</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Duty officer Stanislav Petrov saw satellite warnings of US missile launches and reported them as a malfunction rather than an attack. He was right, and may have averted a nuclear war.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/beirut" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beirut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Truck bombs kill 241 US and 58 French troops in Beirut</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Suicide bombers drove explosive-laden trucks into the US Marine barracks and a French paratroop building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. The attacks led to the withdrawal of the multinational peacekeeping force from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/viernes-negro">Viernes Negro</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Viernes Negro, or Black Friday, was 18 February 1983, when the Venezuelan government of Luis Herrera Campins devalued the bolívar and introduced exchange controls after falling oil prices and heavy foreign debt had drained the reserves.</p></div></li></ol></section>
